{"hash_algorithm":"sha256","ts":"2026-11-02T15:39:10.262Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"6da2f5f4d31f8f762cde8c96","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":{"value":"implementer","source":"argv"},"topic":null,"version":{"value":"2.1.316","source":"probe"}},"op":"create","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/id","value":"pm-4yxzvd"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/title","value":"pm update --unset blocked_by (or --blocked-by none) deletes every blocked_by edge, not only the scalar's: clearing blocker A silently drops an independent prerequisite B"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/description","value":"Reproduced on the feat/write-time-preconditions build (2026.11.2 source). An item with scalar blocked_by=A plus blocked_by edges to A and to an independent prerequisite B: pm update <id> --unset blocked_by leaves blocked_by unset and the dependency list EMPTY. Cause: applyBlockedByMutation clears the scalar and calls reconcileBlockedByDependency(current, undefined), which filters out every kind=blocked_by dependency. The edge to B was never the scalar's. The item then reads as unblocked: next selects it, start admission admits it, and validate no longer flags it, while B is still open. No warning names the dropped edges. Found by the Codex review of PR 1654: pm-urzubi's start refusal had advertised --unset blocked_by as its recovery command; that refusal now advertises it only when the scalar's blocker is the item's only blocked_by edge. Related: pm-scbr0s (the close-time blocker-clear warning names one of N deleted ordering edges) and pm-q6gx (--blocked-by overwrite)."},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/type","value":"Issue"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/status","value":"open"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/priority","value":1},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/tags","value":["agent-ux","area:dependencies","blocked-by","data-loss","graph-integrity"]},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/created_at","value":"2026-11-02T15:39:10.262Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-11-02T15:39:10.262Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/author","value":"harness:claude-code"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/estimated_minutes","value":90},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/acceptance_criteria","value":"Clearing the blocked_by scalar removes at most the edge(s) that point at the scalar's own target, never an independent blocked_by edge; Removing every blocked_by edge requires an explicit, separately named operation (or --clear-deps-like flag) whose receipt lists the removed edges; Regression test: scalar A with edges A and B, unset scalar, B remains and the item stays blocked; The start_blocked_by_open_dependency recovery may then advertise the scalar clear again for multi-edge items"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/parent","value":"pm-33cw"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/risk","value":"medium"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/confidence","value":"high"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/severity","value":"high"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/expected_result","value":"pm update <id> --unset blocked_by clears the scalar and its own edge, keeping independent blocked_by prerequisites"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/actual_result","value":"All blocked_by edges are removed: dependencies goes from [B:blocked_by, A:blocked_by] to [] after --unset blocked_by"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/dependencies","value":[{"id":"pm-q6gx","kind":"related","created_at":"2026-11-02T15:39:10.262Z","author":"harness:claude-code","source_kind":"cli:create:dep","author_source":"detected"},{"id":"pm-scbr0s","kind":"related","created_at":"2026-11-02T15:39:10.262Z","author":"harness:claude-code","source_kind":"cli:create:dep","author_source":"detected"},{"id":"pm-urzubi","kind":"discovered_from","created_at":"2026-11-02T15:39:10.262Z","author":"harness:claude-code","source_kind":"cli:create:dep","author_source":"detected"}]},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/escape_class","value":"production_defect"}],"before_hash":"3cc22dff72be7b14824654a7a64ea62b04799939b2fee54c1b5f52ca60bf6df0","after_hash":"1825f85f898daaadae98c0a0e2c254b33d8218fedafbc8bd36d370dd7856b551","item_hash_version":3,"message":"","context":{"agent_provenance_outcomes":{"model":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"},"version":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"}}},"event_class":"substantive","record_hash_version":1,"record_hash":"6e43cd44878a38e82ba5c14b60249d56ee1e64d79dc62c1c47239def74766fa6"}
{"hash_algorithm":"sha256","ts":"2026-11-02T18:07:32.596Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"6da2f5f4d31f8f762cde8c96","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":null,"topic":null,"version":{"value":"2.1.316","source":"probe"}},"op":"comment_add","patch":[{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-11-02T18:07:32.596Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/comments","value":[{"created_at":"2026-11-02T18:07:32.596Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"Scope evidence from the Codex review round 3 of PR 1654: dependency removal cannot address legacy blocker rows either. --dep-remove normalizes its selector id through normalizeItemReference and compares kinds literally. So a row stored with a short id (missing, normalized to pm-missing), an alias kind (depends_on, which canonicalizes to blocked_by everywhere else) or an external locator without source_kind in the selector (gh:org/repo#12, normalized to pm-gh:org/repo#12) fails with dependency_remove_no_match. Combined with this item's --unset blocked_by data loss, some blockers have no safe single-command removal. pm-urzubi's refusal therefore offers 'pm get <id> --fields blocked_by,dependencies' for them. Fixing this item should make removal selectors canonical (id normalization plus resolveCanonicalRelationshipKind) so recovery can advertise an executable removal again."}]}],"before_hash":"1825f85f898daaadae98c0a0e2c254b33d8218fedafbc8bd36d370dd7856b551","after_hash":"d54a00859d63fe44cbb55b1e5ebcf4c564789770d639ba6bafa6e94e99b7dc9c","item_hash_version":3,"context":{"agent_provenance_outcomes":{"model":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"},"version":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"}}},"event_class":"substantive","record_hash_version":1,"record_hash":"19d3d9041c5d193ed8f9d8400008280a7eb51238f448232ebddc965e08a351e7"}
